course: dictionary
lesson: 10703
title: Unsteady and the clues around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unsteady describes something that is not firm, so it may wobble, shake or fall. «Climbing on unsteady things is a common cause of falls at home.» An unsteady ladder could tip under you, so you should not climb it.
explain: Use the words around the word to check. «Pushing past people instead of saying "excuse me" can knock over older or unsteady people.» Knocked over is a clue: unsteady people are likely to fall. Try your meaning in the sentence: "older people who might fall" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: The same word can describe a voice or a heartbeat that is not regular and keeps changing. An unsteady voice shakes, like a frightened voice in a story. Context tells you which meaning the author means.
fact: Unsteady means not firm, likely to wobble, shake or fall.
fact: Unsteady can also mean not regular or changing a lot, like «an unsteady voice».
fact: The adverb is unsteadily, as in «She walked unsteadily after the long race».
fact: Phrases to know: «unsteady on his feet», «an unsteady hand», «an unsteady ladder».
fact: The opposite of unsteady is steady, which means firm, or regular and calm.
exercise: «Climbing on unsteady things is a common cause of falls at home.» What does unsteady mean here? | Full of happy people | Quiet and still | Very clean and shiny | *Not firm, likely to wobble or fall
exercise: Ella says the chair feels unsteady under her. What should she do? | *Get off it and find something firm to stand on | Shake the chair to test it | Sit down and wait for it to stop | Climb higher to see the view better
exercise: Which sentence uses unsteady to mean not regular or shaking? | The bridge was unsteady after the storm | *Her voice was unsteady as she told the class her news | The table was unsteady, so we put a book under one leg | The ladder was unsteady in the wind
test: Grandad walked unsteadily after his operation. What does unsteadily mean? | Loudly, with a song | Very quickly and happily | *In a wobbly way, not firmly | Slowly, with a book
test: «Pushing past people can knock over unsteady people.» What does this suggest about unsteady people? | They are famous | They are very tall | They are very happy | *They are likely to fall over
exam: Which phrase means someone is not firm on their feet? | an empty cup | a bright, sunny day | *unsteady on his feet | a steady, calm voice
